licious directives. Manual review is recommended.","remediation":"Audit all comments and invisible characters. Remove any instructions that direct the agent to perform unauthorized actions. Use plain, reviewable content.","code_snippet":"  <nav>\n    <a href=\"/\">Home</a>\n    <a href=\"/about\">About</a>\n    <a href=\"/nonexistent-404-page\">Resources</a>  <!-- BUG 1: broken link (404) -->\n  </nav>\n\n  <h1>Widget Dashboard</h1>","intent":null,"tags":["Prompt Injection","llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"37fc8a9e5267037a0132ac4186db0d845e6d77235c70d22248fd9699181ac224","occurrences":[{"file":"browse/test/fixtures/qa-eval.html","start_line":21,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"SDI-1","finding_id":"finding-499bb1dd00c0474485e9d48e17b57978","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.97,"location":{"file":"document-release/SKILL.md","start_line":347,"end_line":402},"finding":null,"explanation":"The skill description frames the task as documentation updating with optional version bumping, but later instructs the agent to commit, push, and edit the PR body automatically. These are materially broader release-management actions with external side effects, and users may not expect remote publication from the stated description alone.","remediation":"Explicitly disclose commit, push, and PR-edit behavior in the skill metadata and require user confirmation before any remote-affecting action. Consider splitting publication steps into a separate skill or making them opt-in flags.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"document-release/SKILL.md","start_line":347,"end_line":402}]},{"id":"TM1","finding_id":"finding-1398441debac40cb888d932dce4a267f","category":"Tool Misuse","pattern":"Tool Parameter Abuse","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.85,"location":{"file":"document-release/SKILL.md","start_line":397,"end_line":null},"finding":"rm -f /tmp/gstack-pr-body-$$.md","explanation":"Tool parameters are crafted to achieve unintended or unsafe behavior. Parameter abuse can bypass intended safety checks (e.g. shell=True, --force, dangerous glob patterns).","remediation":"Validate all tool parameters against an allowlist. Reject dangerous parameter values (shell=True, --force, -rf /) and use safe defaults.","code_snippet":"5. Clean up the tempfile:\n\n```bash\nrm -f /tmp/gstack-pr-body-$$.md\n```\n\n6. If `gh pr view` fails (no PR exists): skip with message \"No PR found — skipping body update.\"","intent":null,"tags":["Tool Misuse","llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"e8ba2b02e857bcc8d0ac6e5ed3ecd85e70a2b89693f1658cb07dbd953137ad0d","occurrences":[{"file":"document-release/SKILL.md","start_line":397,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"TM1","finding_id":"finding-1c4f05598ead4c289a193787080fb302","category":"Tool Misuse","pattern":"Tool Parameter Abuse","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.97,"location":{"file":"gstack-upgrade/SKILL.md","start_line":116,"end_line":null},"finding":"git reset --hard","explanation":"git reset --hard origin/main forcibly discards tracked local changes in the install repository after only performing a generic stash attempt. This is destructive and risky because stash may not preserve everything users expect, and the skill does not require strong confirmation before wiping repository state.","remediation":"Prompt the user with a clear warning before any hard reset, show detected local changes, and offer safer alternatives such as fetch plus rebase/pull or creating a backup branch. Abort on stash failure and provide an automatic restoration path if subsequent setup fails.","code_snippet":"cd \"$INSTALL_DIR\"\nSTASH_OUTPUT=$(git stash 2>&1)\ngit fetch origin\ngit reset --hard origin/main\n./setup\n```\nIf `$STASH_OUTPUT` contains \"Saved working directory\", warn the user: \"Note: local changes were stashed. Run `git stash pop` in the skill directory to restore them.\"","intent":null,"tags":["Tool Misuse"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"f21f5124192a6ca491d13034d2b1dcaae69642decbd659201773b11a2bc5c2a5","occurrences":[{"file":"gstack-upgrade/SKILL.md","start_line":116,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"TM1","finding_id":"finding-bea4e24d61f34c4aaf9019e48c78d9d7","category":"Tool Misuse","pattern":"Tool Parameter Abuse","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.94,"location":{"file":"gstack-upgrade/SKILL.md","start_line":153,"end_line":null},"finding":"rm -rf \"$LOCAL_GSTACK/.git\"","explanation":"The command recursively deletes .git in the local vendored copy after copying files into it. Even though the path is quoted, this intentionally destroys repository metadata and history, which is a high-impact destructive action that can remove provenance and local work if the target is misidentified or contains meaningful VCS state.","remediation":"Do not delete .git automatically. If removing VCS metadata is necessary, prompt the user explicitly, verify the target is a disposable vendored copy, and preserve a restorable backup until the entire sync succeeds.","code_snippet":"```bash\nmv \"$LOCAL_GSTACK\" \"$LOCAL_GSTACK.bak\"\ncp -Rf \"$INSTALL_DIR\" \"$LOCAL_GSTACK\"\nrm -rf \"$LOCAL_GSTACK/.git\"\ncd \"$LOCAL_GSTACK\" && ./setup\nrm -rf \"$LOCAL_GSTACK.bak\"\n```","intent":null,"tags":["Tool Misuse"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"d19dbfc374b967e58d0fd6908b8167b68d9dc3c52d94a04c24c276102212c63a","occurrences":[{"file":"gstack-upgrade/SKILL.md","start_line":153,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"TM1","finding_id":"finding-586fa38c2a7841be89d9e30ae2ce9d19","category":"Tool Misuse","pattern":"Tool Parameter Abuse","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.85,"location":{"file":"gstack-upgrade/SKILL.md","start_line":164,"end_line":null},"finding":"rm -f ~/.gstack/last-update-check","explanation":"Tool parameters are crafted to achieve unintended or unsafe behavior. Parameter abuse can bypass intended safety checks (e.g. shell=True, --force, dangerous glob patterns).","remediation":"Validate all tool parameters against an allowlist. Reject dangerous parameter values (shell=True, --force, -rf /) and use safe defaults.","code_snippet":"```bash\nmkdir -p ~/.gstack\necho \"$OLD_VERSION\" > ~/.gstack/just-upgraded-from\nrm -f ~/.gstack/last-update-check\nrm -f ~/.gstack/update-snoozed\n```\n","intent":null,"tags":["Tool Misuse","llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"1434d4c26141ac8f0efe3d9b5d61b2735562ae2a8abe3c99ad4d9bc647da2c97","occurrences":[{"file":"gstack-upgrade/SKILL.md","start_line":164,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"TM1","finding_id":"finding-326ff24328b842ebbc4f8e574ecabf9d","category":"Tool Misuse","pattern":"Tool Parameter Abuse","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.85,"location":{"file":"gstack-upgrade/SKILL.md","start_line":165,"end_line":null},"finding":"rm -f ~/.gstack/update-snoozed","explanation":"Tool parameters are crafted to achieve unintended or unsafe behavior. Parameter abuse can bypass intended safety checks (e.g. shell=True, --force, dangerous glob patterns).","remediation":"Validate all tool parameters against an allowlist. Reject dangerous parameter values (shell=True, --force, -rf /) and use safe defaults.","code_snippet":"mkdir -p ~/.gstack\necho \"$OLD_VERSION\" > ~/.gstack/just-upgraded-from\nrm -f ~/.gstack/last-update-check\nrm -f ~/.gstack/update-snoozed\n```\n\n### Step 6: Show What's New","intent":null,"tags":["Tool Misuse","llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"b2dce93791e438f8dcdf9c611f14dbc0fa33704a05235a52e32283e4428938a0","occurrences":[{"file":"gstack-upgrade/SKILL.md","start_line":165,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"TM2","finding_id":"finding-1a65599af8674c35960905c273e4372e","category":"Tool Misuse","pattern":"Chaining Abuse","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.75,"location":{"file":"package.json","start_line":11,"end_line":null},"finding":"&& rm -","explanation":"Tool calls are chained to bypass individual safety checks or escalate capabilities beyond what any single tool call would allow.","remediation":"Limit tool chaining depth and validate the output of each tool before passing it to the next. Require explicit user approval for multi-step chains.","code_snippet":"    \"browse\": \"./browse/dist/browse\"\n  },\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"build\": \"bun run gen:skill-docs && bun build --compile browse/src/cli.ts --outfile browse/dist/browse && bun build --compile browse/src/find-browse.ts --outfile browse/dist/find-browse && git rev-parse HEAD > browse/dist/.version && rm -f .*.bun-build\",\n    \"gen:skill-docs\": \"bun run scripts/gen-skill-docs.ts\",\n    \"dev\": \"bun run browse/src/cli.ts\",\n    \"server\": \"bun run browse/src/server.ts\",","intent":null,"tags":["Tool Misuse","llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"da42bed49ba667f4edac8a3628fa28e5cc6bbc20fc757fb7d1ddbddd9a531ec0","occurrences":[{"file":"package.json","start_line":11,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"P2","finding_id":"finding-7587fe4d3c57494baea0e46eff822a4a","category":"Prompt Injection","pattern":"Hidden Instructions","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.91,"location":{"file":"plan-ceo-review/SKILL.md","start_line":16,"end_line":null},"finding":"<!-- AUTO-GENERATED from SKILL.md.tmpl — do not edit directly -->\n<!-- Regenerate: bun run gen:skill-docs -->\n\n## Preamble (run first)\n\n```bash\n_UPD=$(~/.claude/skills/gstack/bin/gstack-update-check 2","explanation":"The skill contains hidden operational instructions in comments and a mandatory preamble that an agent may follow without the user understanding the resulting behavior. Hidden or non-obvious instructions are dangerous in agent workflows because they can smuggle side effects, persistence, or policy changes into an apparently benign review skill.","remediation":"Eliminate hidden instructions and move all operational behavior into visible, concise, user-auditable sections. Any mandatory setup with side effects should be explicitly described in metadata and limited to the minimum necessary behavior.","code_snippet":"  - Bash\n  - AskUserQuestion\n---\n<!-- AUTO-GENERATED from SKILL.md.tmpl — do not edit directly -->\n<!-- Regenerate: bun run gen:skill-docs -->\n\n## Preamble (run first)","intent":null,"tags":["Prompt Injection"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"3a0c5ffa5c69502d128bf64dcffb72ee0e79416095ee1d74c813e8d8dea52750","occurrences":[{"file":"plan-ceo-review/SKILL.md","start_line":16,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"SDI-3","finding_id":"finding-6934e6079b9442aaa3d38149ed73441b","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.99,"location":{"file":"plan-ceo-review/SKILL.md","start_line":22,"end_line":30},"finding":null,"explanation":"The documented workflow exceeds a read-only review scope by instructing shell commands that write files, delete files, run git/gh operations, and potentially influence remote repository state in later steps. In a review skill, such side effects violate least privilege and can cause unintended local state changes or remote actions that the user did not authorize.","remediation":"Constrain the skill to read-only commands unless the user explicitly approves state-changing actions at runtime. Remove file creation/deletion from the preamble, forbid remote-affecting git/gh commands in the skill body, and split any mutation-capable workflow into a separate skill with prominently disclosed permissions.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"plan-ceo-review/SKILL.md","start_line":22,"end_line":30}]},{"id":"SC2","finding_id":"finding-7dd6f4aa3e474dceb37c0f746cec599e","category":"Supply Chain","pattern":"External Script Fetching","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.99,"location":{"file":"qa-only/SKILL.md","start_line":120,"end_line":null},"finding":"curl -fsSL https://bun.sh/install | bash","explanation":"This line enables immediate arbitrary code execution from an external source by piping fetched content directly into `bash`. In the context of an agent skill, that is especially dangerous because the action may be performed as part of an otherwise routine workflow, increasing the chance of unsafe execution with user trust.","remediation":"Remove the curl-to-shell install path entirely. Replace it with documented manual installation steps or package-manager commands, and if the skill must verify prerequisites, have it stop and ask the user to install them independently.","code_snippet":"If `NEEDS_SETUP`:\n1. Tell the user: \"gstack browse needs a one-time build (~10 seconds). OK to proceed?\" Then STOP and wait.\n2. Run: `cd <SKILL_DIR> && ./setup`\n3. If `bun` is not installed: `curl -fsSL https://bun.sh/install | bash`\n\n**Create output directories:**\n","intent":null,"tags":["Supply Chain"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"e7c1bd5dac6d6024151481e2c2ae15f435382f837912f64adae5d7e7c01042c0","occurrences":[{"file":"qa-only/SKILL.md","start_line":120,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"SDI-4","finding_id":"finding-6966fdf0eea14630b0bcd8d7a88c4398","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"location":{"file":"qa-only/SKILL.md","start_line":151,"end_line":182},"finding":null,"explanation":"The documentation repeatedly frames this as report-only black-box QA and explicitly says 'Never read source code' and 'NEVER fix anything.' However, the diff-aware workflow directs the agent to inspect changed files, infer routes from controllers/models/services, read commit messages/PR descriptions, and consult TODOS.md, which is source- and repo-level analysis rather than user-style QA.","remediation":"Remove the repository/code-inspection steps from /qa-only, or update the skill documentation to explicitly state that diff-aware mode performs code and repo artifact inspection to scope testing. If black-box QA is the intent, limit scope discovery to runtime navigation and user-provided context only.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":["llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"qa-only/SKILL.md","start_line":151,"end_line":182}]},{"id":"SDI-1","finding_id":"finding-0b590f3c61014c73b0e5281987970007","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"location":{"file":"qa/SKILL.md","start_line":5,"end_line":10},"finding":null,"explanation":"The skill is presented as browser-based QA, but its workflow escalates into source-code modification, git commits, TODO updates, and cross-session artifact writes. That mismatch can cause an operator to invoke a seemingly observational skill that actually performs durable code and repository changes, increasing the chance of unintended or unauthorized actions.","remediation":"Split this into separate skills: a read-only QA/testing skill and an explicit fix-and-commit remediation skill. Add prominent upfront warnings in the metadata and description that the skill may edit source, create commits, and write persistent artifacts, and require explicit user confirmation before any write, commit, or out-of-repo action.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"qa/SKILL.md","start_line":5,"end_line":10}]},{"id":"SQP-1","finding_id":"finding-fc2344d0e52144b98e55afbd727bafca","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.95,"location":{"file":"qa/SKILL.md","start_line":7,"end_line":10},"finding":null,"explanation":"Trigger phrases such as 'test this site,' 'find bugs,' and 'fix what's broken' are broad and likely to match ordinary requests where the user may not expect code edits, commits, or persistent artifact writes. Overbroad activation increases the chance of accidental invocation of a high-impact workflow.","remediation":"Narrow the trigger phrases to explicit intents like 'qa and auto-fix with commits' or 'run QA remediation workflow.' Require a confirmation gate before any non-read-only behavior begins, especially when the prompt did not explicitly request edits or commits.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"qa/SKILL.md","start_line":7,"end_line":10}]},{"id":"SDI-2","finding_id":"finding-e2c20911a8a3410badd11742b6b707b2","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"location":{"file":"qa/SKILL.md","start_line":26,"end_line":37},"finding":null,"explanation":"The preamble introduces upgrade logic unrelated to QA execution, including reading and following another skill's upgrade instructions and persisting snooze state. This expands the trust boundary from QA steps to self-modifying tooling behavior, which could be abused to steer the agent into additional actions the user did not request.","remediation":"Remove inline upgrade handling from the QA skill entirely, or move it to a separate, explicitly invoked maintenance workflow. At minimum, disable automatic upgrade-following and require explicit user consent before reading secondary skill files or changing local tool state.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"qa/SKILL.md","start_line":26,"end_line":37}]},{"id":"SC2","finding_id":"finding-be1e5f2564c245a2a589404f7e89fb41","category":"Supply Chain","pattern":"External Script Fetching","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.99,"location":{"file":"qa/SKILL.md","start_line":158,"end_line":null},"finding":"curl -fsSL https://bun.sh/install | bash","explanation":"The skill instructs piping a remote script directly into bash to install bun. This is a classic remote code execution pattern: any compromise of the delivery path, installer, or DNS/TLS context would execute arbitrary code on the host under the agent's privileges.","remediation":"Never use curl-pipe-to-shell in a skill. Replace it with a pinned, checksum-verified installation process from a trusted package manager or a manually reviewed installer file, and require explicit user approval before any software installation.","code_snippet":"If `NEEDS_SETUP`:\n1. Tell the user: \"gstack browse needs a one-time build (~10 seconds). OK to proceed?\" Then STOP and wait.\n2. Run: `cd <SKILL_DIR> && ./setup`\n3. If `bun` is not installed: `curl -fsSL https://bun.sh/install | bash`\n\n**Create output directories:**\n","intent":null,"tags":["Supply Chain"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"e7c1bd5dac6d6024151481e2c2ae15f435382f837912f64adae5d7e7c01042c0","occurrences":[{"file":"qa/SKILL.md","start_line":158,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"SDI-4","finding_id":"finding-a98b3e65bec34ab69c001e0da16e4e19","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.99,"location":{"file":"qa/SKILL.md","start_line":451,"end_line":451},"finding":null,"explanation":"The skill says 'Never read source code' but later requires locating, reading, and editing source to fix issues. This contradiction is dangerous because it obscures the real behavior of the skill and makes operator review harder, increasing the risk that invasive actions occur under misleading guardrails.","remediation":"Remove the contradictory instruction and replace it with a precise phase distinction: user-mode testing during discovery, then explicit developer-mode source inspection during remediation. Clearly state when the workflow transitions from observational QA to code modification.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"qa/SKILL.md","start_line":451,"end_line":451}]},{"id":"SDI-1","finding_id":"finding-a8ae4ac5f7574cd1a70f216227851c55","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.99,"location":{"file":"retro/SKILL.md","start_line":46,"end_line":81},"finding":null,"explanation":"The contributor-mode section instructs the agent to create tooling field reports in ~/.gstack/contributor-logs, which is unrelated to generating an engineering retrospective and is not disclosed in the skill description. This can cause silent exfiltration-like persistence of workflow details and user activity into global files the user did not expect this skill to write.","remediation":"Delete contributor-mode reporting from the retro skill, or split it into a separate explicitly opt-in feedback skill. If feedback collection is retained, require clear user consent before writing any report and disclose the destination, contents, and trigger conditions in the skill metadata.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"retro/SKILL.md","start_line":46,"end_line":81}]},{"id":"SDI-2","finding_id":"finding-84f65225d549487290f4cafe378fddba","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.99,"location":{"file":"retro/SKILL.md","start_line":50,"end_line":81},"finding":null,"explanation":"The instructions explicitly direct writes to ~/.gstack/contributor-logs for field reports, which is not justified by the skill's retrospective purpose. This creates persistent global artifacts containing operational details and potentially sensitive context, outside the project and without clear user awareness.","remediation":"Eliminate home-directory writes from the retrospective workflow. If product feedback is desired, prompt the user for explicit approval and store reports in a clearly documented, user-chosen location with data minimization.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"retro/SKILL.md","start_line":50,"end_line":81}]},{"id":"AR2","finding_id":"finding-f77bd1398cf94f808c74eb32ca8a29a8","category":"Anti-Refusal","pattern":"Anti-Refusal Statement","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.8,"location":{"file":"retro/SKILL.md","start_line":499,"end_line":null},"finding":"without judgment","explanation":"Skill instructs the agent to omit warnings, disclaimers, or ethical commentary. Stripping safety caveats hides risk from the user and is a common jailbreak preamble.","remediation":"Remove instructions that suppress warnings, disclaimers, or ethical commentary. Let the agent surface safety-relevant caveats to the user.","code_snippet":"**AI collaboration note:** If many commits have `Co-Authored-By` AI trailers (e.g., Claude, Copilot), note the AI-assisted commit percentage as a team metric. Frame it neutrally — \"N% of commits were AI-assisted\" — without judgment.","intent":null,"tags":["Anti-Refusal","contextual-triage","likely-benign-context","llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"6aeb7bdd33a2d47e97f612d4064bfee037a399e14f8e408724067422a6e7f617","occurrences":[{"file":"retro/SKILL.md","start_line":499,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"SDI-4","finding_id":"finding-562ce9498f9e42aabc68479d8b68bd3d","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.99,"location":{"file":"retro/SKILL.md","start_line":539,"end_line":543},"finding":null,"explanation":"The skill states that the only file written is the retro JSON snapshot, but elsewhere instructs additional writes for contributor reports and upgrade snooze state. This contradiction is dangerous because it misleads reviewers and users about actual side effects, making hidden persistence harder to detect and audit.","remediation":"Make file-write behavior internally consistent. Either remove all extra writes or update the skill to enumerate every write path and purpose, with explicit user consent for non-essential writes.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"retro/SKILL.md","start_line":539,"end_line":543}]},{"id":"SDI-2","finding_id":"finding-fac59b5ddd814b5ebca0c40258d0d8e0","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"location":{"file":"review/SKILL.md","start_line":33,"end_line":33},"finding":null,"explanation":"The skill tells the agent to manage upgrades, read another skill file, possibly auto-upgrade, and persist snooze state even though none of that is required for PR review. This creates unjustified write actions and control-flow redirection into external instructions, which is dangerous because a review task should not mutate local tool state or execute maintenance workflows implicitly.","remediation":"Remove inline upgrade flow handling from the review skill entirely. Require upgrades to be performed only through a dedicated maintenance command with explicit user approval, and prohibit persistent state writes from review-only workflows.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"review/SKILL.md","start_line":33,"end_line":33}]},{"id":"SDI-4","finding_id":"finding-eb79f5278d7e4a299f02d3afd2603193","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.95,"location":{"file":"review/SKILL.md","start_line":97,"end_line":99},"finding":null,"explanation":"The skill contains contradictory safety boundaries: it claims to be read-only and forbids PR creation, push, and commit, yet earlier instructions require comment interactions and mention gh PR operations. Conflicting instructions in an agent skill are dangerous because they can cause unsafe action selection and weaken operator expectations about whether the workflow will modify remote state.","remediation":"Resolve the contradiction by making the action policy explicit and consistent throughout the document. If remote comment/reply actions are allowed, declare them clearly and narrowly; otherwise remove all instructions that post comments or invoke PR-management flows.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"review/SKILL.md","start_line":97,"end_line":99}]},{"id":"SDI-2","finding_id":"finding-cebc50bedc0c4096a62dbb3d4f6d139f","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"location":{"file":"review/greptile-triage.md","start_line":7,"end_line":27},"finding":null,"explanation":"These instructions add GitHub API-based fetching, filtering, and classification of PR comments to a skill advertised as a headless browser for QA testing. That mismatch broadens the operational scope into repository intelligence gathering and review automation, which can be abused to influence code review outcomes or access repository metadata under false pretenses.","remediation":"Constrain the skill to browser QA tasks only, or move GitHub review triage into a separately named and permissioned skill. Align the manifest description with actual capabilities and require explicit authorization for repository/API access outside browser testing.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"review/greptile-triage.md","start_line":7,"end_line":27}]},{"id":"SDI-1","finding_id":"finding-7e70449a5afa49ecb9e7936613c85592","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.97,"location":{"file":"review/greptile-triage.md","start_line":76,"end_line":93},"finding":null,"explanation":"This section instructs the skill to post replies to GitHub PR comments, which is an external side effect unrelated to the declared browser-testing scope of the gstack skill. A user invoking a QA/browser tool would not reasonably expect it to classify review comments and write back to GitHub, creating a capability mismatch that can lead to unauthorized repository interactions or workflow manipulation.","remediation":"Remove PR comment reply behavior from this skill, or split it into a separate explicitly declared GitHub-triage skill with clear user-facing scope and consent. Ensure the manifest accurately declares write-capable GitHub actions and require explicit approval before any comment-posting operation.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"review/greptile-triage.md","start_line":76,"end_line":93}]},{"id":"SC2","finding_id":"finding-227cce25fd07433ba1e9d259bf89b36e","category":"Supply Chain","pattern":"External Script Fetching","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.97,"location":{"file":"scripts/gen-skill-docs.ts","start_line":182,"end_line":null},"finding":"curl -fsSL https://bun.sh/install | bash","explanation":"The generated setup instructions tell the agent to install Bun via 'curl ... | bash', which executes a remotely fetched script without verification. If the transport endpoint, upstream hosting, DNS, or distribution channel is compromised, arbitrary shell commands would run with the user's privileges.","remediation":"Replace pipe-to-shell installation with a safer, verified process. Prefer package-manager installation, or download a pinned release artifact and verify checksum/signature before execution; additionally, require explicit user confirmation before any install step and document the trust assumptions.","code_snippet":"If \\`NEEDS_SETUP\\`:\n1. Tell the user: \"gstack browse needs a one-time build (~10 seconds). OK to proceed?\" Then STOP and wait.\n2. Run: \\`cd <SKILL_DIR> && ./setup\\`\n3. If \\`bun\\` is not installed: \\`curl -fsSL https://bun.sh/install | bash\\``;\n}\n\nfunction generateBaseBranchDetect(): string {","intent":null,"tags":["Supply Chain"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"e7c1bd5dac6d6024151481e2c2ae15f435382f837912f64adae5d7e7c01042c0","occurrences":[{"file":"scripts/gen-skill-docs.ts","start_line":182,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"YR1","finding_id":"finding-5136a06e132b4a4980a228360728e0e1","category":"YARA Match","pattern":"YARA rule 'info_stealer': Information stealer patterns (credential harvesting, browser data theft) [malware]","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.75,"location":{"file":"setup-browser-cookies/SKILL.md","start_line":5,"end_line":null},"finding":"cookies from your real browser (Comet, Chrome, Arc, Brave, Edge","explanation":"YARA rule matched a known malware signature (reverse shell, backdoor, ransomware, C2 framework, or info stealer).","remediation":"Remove the malware payload or compromised file entirely. Investigate how it entered the skill and audit all other artifacts for additional indicators of compromise.","code_snippet":"---\nname: setup-browser-cookies\nversion: 1.0.0\ndescription: |\n  Import cookies from your real browser (Comet, Chrome, Arc, Brave, Edge) into the\n  headless browse session. Opens an interactive picker UI where you select which\n  cookie domains to import. Use before QA testing authenticated pages.\nallowed-tools:\n  - Bash\n  - Read\n  - AskUserQuestion\n---\n<!-- AUTO-GENERATED from SKILL.md.tmpl — do not edit directly -->\n<!-- Regenerate: bun run gen:skill-docs -->\n\n## Preamble (run first)\n\n```bash\n_UPD=$(~/.claude/skills/gstack/bin/gstack-update-check 2>/dev/null || .claude/skills/gstack/bin/gstack-update-check 2>/dev/null || true)\n[ -n \"$_UPD\" ] && echo \"$_UPD\" || ","intent":null,"tags":["YARA Match","llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"c5ef4860a304a730c48e3dfd09a0a54d9e9d1e65536f40def09aba4251bcc3a8","occurrences":[{"file":"setup-browser-cookies/SKILL.md","start_line":5,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"SDI-2","finding_id":"finding-ca1cc7266efd49439c4e8b8ffa695864","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"location":{"file":"setup-browser-cookies/SKILL.md","start_line":110,"end_line":113},"finding":null,"explanation":"The setup path authorizes installing external software to support the skill, including a remote bootstrap path for Bun. Allowing dependency installation from within a cookie-import skill materially increases risk because the workflow already handles authenticated session material, so adding software installation broadens compromise potential far beyond the stated purpose.","remediation":"Do not install dependencies automatically from within the skill. Require preinstalled, verified dependencies; provide separate setup documentation; and if installation is unavoidable, use signed packages or pinned checksums with explicit user approval in a distinct setup flow.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"setup-browser-cookies/SKILL.md","start_line":110,"end_line":113}]},{"id":"SC2","finding_id":"finding-3318981cc8be467aac2312c1f34adbfa","category":"Supply Chain","pattern":"External Script Fetching","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.99,"location":{"file":"setup-browser-cookies/SKILL.md","start_line":113,"end_line":null},"finding":"curl -fsSL https://bun.sh/install | bash","explanation":"Piping a remote script directly into bash executes unverified code from the network with the user's privileges. In a skill that handles browser cookies and authenticated sessions, this is especially dangerous because a compromised installer or man-in-the-middle style failure could immediately lead to credential theft, persistence, or full local compromise.","remediation":"Replace curl-pipe-shell installation with a separately documented manual setup step using trusted package managers, signature verification, or checksum-pinned artifacts. Never execute remote installer scripts inline inside a security-sensitive skill.","code_snippet":"If `NEEDS_SETUP`:\n1. Tell the user: \"gstack browse needs a one-time build (~10 seconds). OK to proceed?\" Then STOP and wait.\n2. Run: `cd <SKILL_DIR> && ./setup`\n3. If `bun` is not installed: `curl -fsSL https://bun.sh/install | bash`\n\n### 2. Open the cookie picker\n","intent":null,"tags":["Supply Chain"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"e7c1bd5dac6d6024151481e2c2ae15f435382f837912f64adae5d7e7c01042c0","occurrences":[{"file":"setup-browser-cookies/SKILL.md","start_line":113,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"SDI-1","finding_id":"finding-f9fbecb6af864cc0ab5d281865c74b75","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.99,"location":{"file":"ship/SKILL.md","start_line":4,"end_line":5},"finding":null,"explanation":"The skill metadata and surrounding product context describe a headless browser QA tool, but this file defines a fully automated git/GitHub shipping workflow that merges branches, commits, pushes, and creates PRs. This mismatch is dangerous because a user or calling system may grant trust and permissions appropriate for browser testing while the skill actually performs repository-changing actions.","remediation":"Ensure the skill is accurately named, scoped, and documented as a release/shipping workflow rather than a browser-testing tool. Separate browser QA capabilities from repository mutation workflows, require explicit user opt-in before any git push/PR creation actions, and enforce policy checks so tool descriptions match actual behavior.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"ship/SKILL.md","start_line":4,"end_line":5}]},{"id":"SDI-2","finding_id":"finding-a4f0d86a75f941de8bdf5bbead5aacb3","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"location":{"file":"ship/SKILL.md","start_line":102,"end_line":142},"finding":null,"explanation":"The skill performs repository mutation and remote GitHub actions including merge, version bump, changelog edits, commits, pushes, and PR creation, which are unrelated to the stated browser-testing purpose. In context, this expands the authority of a seemingly benign QA skill into source-control automation, increasing the risk of unauthorized code changes and remote publication.","remediation":"Remove git/GitHub mutation steps from this skill or move them into a separately named and permission-scoped shipping skill. Add explicit capability declarations, require interactive confirmation before merge/commit/push/PR actions, and gate remote operations behind least-privilege approvals.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"ship/SKILL.md","start_line":102,"end_line":142}]},{"id":"SDI-2","finding_id":"finding-1bb1f3f2522244fea75b3379faa4e7da","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"location":{"file":"test/helpers/session-runner.ts","start_line":143,"end_line":158},"finding":null,"explanation":"This helper launches `claude` through `sh -c` and explicitly passes `--dangerously-skip-permissions`, removing an important safety boundary for a subprocess that consumes prompt-controlled input. Even though this is framed as test infrastructure, it enables the spawned agent to invoke powerful tools without approval, which is especially risky in a browser-testing skill where prompts, pages, or test fixtures may be adversarial.","remediation":"Remove `--dangerously-skip-permissions` and run the CLI with normal permission enforcement. Avoid `sh -c` entirely by spawning `claude` directly with an argument array and piping stdin normally; if stdin buffering is an issue, use a direct file descriptor/stream rather than a shell pipeline. Restrict allowed tools to the minimum required for the test and isolate execution in a sandboxed temp directory/container.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"test/helpers/session-runner.ts","start_line":143,"end_line":158}]},{"id":"AS3","finding_id":"finding-8fc74724ae9c4e59a5e0dc00404f7e3a","category":"Agent Snooping","pattern":"Skill Enumeration","severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.85,"location":{"file":"ARCHITECTURE.md","start_line":42,"end_line":null},"finding":"ls into `~/.claude/skills","explanation":"Skill enumerates or reads other installed skills. Access to other skills' SKILL.md files or the skills directory reveals prompt instructions, capabilities, and secrets that should be invisible to peer skills.","remediation":"Remove all code or instructions that list or read other skills' files or directories. Skills should operate independently; cross-skill access is a privilege escalation.","code_snippet":"\nNode.js would work. Bun is better here for three reasons:\n\n1. **Compiled binaries.** `bun build --compile` produces a single ~58MB executable. No `node_modules` at runtime, no `npx`, no PATH configuration. The binary just runs. This matters because gstack installs into `~/.claude/skills/` where users don't expect to manage a Node.js project.\n\n2. **Native SQLite.** Cookie decryption reads Chromium's SQLite cookie database directly. Bun has `new Database()` built in — no `better-sqlite3`, no native addon compilation, no gyp. One less thing that breaks on different machines.\n","intent":null,"tags":["Agent Snooping","llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"60e4c684103d831b0df20d481b19fd4c96cd4c368f67957faa04a4dd8943a7f6","occurrences":[{"file":"ARCHITECTURE.md","start_line":42,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"SDI-1","finding_id":"finding-a288c1a3f36e4edfaf9d5781589c29f6","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.88,"location":{"file":"ARCHITECTURE.md","start_line":44,"end_line":45},"finding":null,"explanation":"The manifest describes a fast headless browser for QA, navigation, interaction, screenshots, layout checks, form testing, dialogs, and assertions. This document additionally describes direct reading and decryption of Chromium's cookie database, which is a materially broader capability than normal browser automation and is not mentioned in the manifest.","remediation":"Update the manifest to explicitly disclose cookie import/decryption capability if it is intended, including why it is needed for QA workflows, or remove/segregate this functionality into a separately described skill.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":["llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"ARCHITECTURE.md","start_line":44,"end_line":45}]},{"id":"RA2","finding_id":"finding-a9a5ba98c38e428eb771f38636defbac","category":"Rogue Agent","pattern":"Session Persistence","severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.6,"location":{"file":"ARCHITECTURE.md","start_line":334,"end_line":null},"finding":"write `.tmp`, `fs.renameSync`). Survives kills.\n2. **Final:** `finalize()` writes a timestamped eval file (e.g. `e2e-20260314-143022.json`). The partial file is never cleaned up — it persists alongsid","explanation":"Skill establishes unauthorized persistence across sessions via cron jobs, startup scripts, or state files. Session persistence allows an attacker to maintain access beyond the current interaction.","remediation":"Remove any persistence mechanisms (cron jobs, startup scripts, state files). Skills should not maintain state across sessions without explicit user consent.","code_snippet":"\nThe `EvalCollector` accumulates test results and writes them in two ways:\n\n1. **Incremental:** `savePartial()` writes `_partial-e2e.json` after each test (atomic: write `.tmp`, `fs.renameSync`). Survives kills.\n2. **Final:** `finalize()` writes a timestamped eval file (e.g. `e2e-20260314-143022.json`). The partial file is never cleaned up — it persists alongside the final file for observability.\n\n`eval:compare` diffs two eval runs. `eval:summary` aggregates stats across all runs in `~/.gstack-dev/evals/`.","intent":null,"tags":["Rogue Agent","llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"83899b0a429dc10f8b5567e94f721ae96f55ab4a4be73f78f3fac7ccb39ef8d7","occurrences":[{"file":"ARCHITECTURE.md","start_line":334,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"SDI-1","finding_id":"finding-c4b64833ba764be4973d10d6e2d7a322","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.93,"location":{"file":"BROWSER.md","start_line":18,"end_line":18},"finding":null,"explanation":"The documented `cookie-import` and `cookie-import-browser` capabilities extend the tool from ordinary QA browsing into importing existing authenticated browser state. That creates a clear credential/session access surface: an agent using this skill could assume a user's web sessions for unrelated services, which is materially more sensitive than normal test automation.","remediation":"Restrict cookie import to explicit opt-in flows with prominent warnings and per-import confirmation. Limit imports to user-specified domains or dedicated test profiles, disable real-browser cookie decryption by default, and add audit logging so any session import is visible and reviewable.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"BROWSER.md","start_line":18,"end_line":18}]},{"id":"SQP-2","finding_id":"finding-5204c787ed3346b492fc3d4c25a6ce0f","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.91,"location":{"file":"BROWSER.md","start_line":18,"end_line":18},"finding":null,"explanation":"Sensitive cookie import is documented without strong user-facing warnings about accessing existing authenticated sessions. This omission increases the likelihood of unsafe use because users and agents may treat the feature as routine QA behavior rather than credential-bearing functionality.","remediation":"Add explicit warnings in the docs and CLI help that cookie import may access sensitive authenticated state. Require affirmative confirmation before importing cookies and recommend use of separate test profiles instead of personal browser data.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"BROWSER.md","start_line":18,"end_line":18}]},{"id":"SDI-4","finding_id":"finding-5ea8dbf4f1c0480484a853c0d0c83951","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.98,"location":{"file":"BROWSER.md","start_line":88,"end_line":88},"finding":null,"explanation":"The snapshot section states 'No injected scripts. Just Playwright's native accessibility API.' However, the very next extended features section documents `--annotate` injecting temporary overlay divs into the page before screenshotting. That is an active contradiction in the documentation about whether page injection occurs.","remediation":"Revise the statement to qualify that normal snapshotting avoids injection, except for modes like `--annotate` that temporarily inject overlays for visualization.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":["llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"BROWSER.md","start_line":88,"end_line":88}]},{"id":"PE2","finding_id":"finding-35b9254db0fc48579182c5faee56e549","category":"Privilege Escalation","pattern":"Sudo/Root Execution","severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.8,"location":{"file":"BROWSER.md","start_line":114,"end_line":null},"finding":"chmod 600","explanation":"Commands invoke sudo or root privileges. Verify this elevated access is necessary and justified.","remediation":"Avoid sudo/root unless strictly required. Prefer least-privilege patterns. If elevation is needed, document the justification and scope.","code_snippet":"\n### Authentication\n\nEach server session generates a random UUID as a bearer token. The token is written to the state file (`.gstack/browse.json`) with chmod 600. Every HTTP request must include `Authorization: Bearer <token>`. This prevents other processes on the machine from controlling the browser.\n\n### Console, network, and dialog capture\n","intent":null,"tags":["Privilege Escalation","llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"fac387638fbcbc4ed94acb702b3cf68cbc839ee694afdddde6d97a39a3523b73","occurrences":[{"file":"BROWSER.md","start_line":114,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"SQP-2","finding_id":"finding-42ce04d447c04fc89a4b450d7e54c1a5","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.88,"location":{"file":"BROWSER.md","start_line":118,"end_line":124},"finding":null,"explanation":"Automatic disk logging of console, network, and dialog events can capture sensitive URLs, headers, tokens, form data, and other application secrets, yet the documentation provides no warning or retention guidance. Persisting this data to `.gstack/` increases exposure beyond transient browser memory.","remediation":"Make disk logging opt-in or redact sensitive fields by default, especially authorization headers, cookies, and request bodies. Document the privacy implications, provide retention controls, and allow users to disable or securely clear logs.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"BROWSER.md","start_line":118,"end_line":124}]},{"id":"SQP-2","finding_id":"finding-e10b6ce8253247068f6ed0bfff5dd19b","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.84,"location":{"file":"BROWSER.md","start_line":128,"end_line":128},"finding":null,"explanation":"Auto-accepting dialogs by default can unintentionally confirm destructive or security-relevant actions such as deletes, purchases, or consent prompts. Without a warning, operators may not realize the browser will approve dialogs rather than pause, which raises the risk of unintended state changes.","remediation":"Change the default to prompt, block, or require explicit per-session opt-in for auto-accept behavior. At minimum, warn clearly in documentation and runtime output when auto-accept is enabled and log each acceptance prominently.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"BROWSER.md","start_line":128,"end_line":128}]},{"id":"SDI-2","finding_id":"finding-60660990b0b04d64a065bc9a5dab4a10","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.87,"location":{"file":"BROWSER.md","start_line":130,"end_line":140},"finding":null,"explanation":"The `js` and `eval` commands allow arbitrary in-page JavaScript execution, which exceeds simple click/fill/read primitives and can invoke privileged page actions, extract data, or alter application state in ways that are hard to constrain. In an agent-operated browser, this broadens the action surface and can bypass higher-level safety assumptions about what the browser tool does.","remediation":"Constrain JavaScript execution by disabling it by default, requiring explicit enablement, or offering a limited safe subset for inspection-only use cases. Add user-visible warnings, permission checks, and logging for any arbitrary script execution.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"BROWSER.md","start_line":130,"end_line":140}]},{"id":"SDI-1","finding_id":"finding-17e37682938f477aac9118527a7349b8","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.95,"location":{"file":"CHANGELOG.md","start_line":5,"end_line":18},"finding":null,"explanation":"The changelog shows the skill has grown far beyond a narrow headless-browser QA tool into a broader agent framework with self-update, repo-aware workflows, evals, and documentation automation. That scope drift matters because users may grant this skill permissions appropriate for browser testing while it also performs repository and maintenance actions they would not reasonably expect from the manifest description.","remediation":"Align the published description and permission model with actual capabilities, or split non-browser features into separate skills. Remove or gate repo-writing, update, and documentation-management features behind explicit user opt-in and capability-specific documentation.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"CHANGELOG.md","start_line":5,"end_line":18}]},{"id":"SDI-2","finding_id":"finding-9575b2140af74ec3802a269f0f8a9764","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.9,"location":{"file":"CHANGELOG.md","start_line":5,"end_line":12},"finding":null,"explanation":"Self-update and GitHub release checking are outside the stated QA-browser purpose and introduce supply-chain and execution-trust risk. A skill that can fetch release state, bypass caches, and trigger upgrades may cause users to execute unreviewed code changes under the assumption they are only using a testing utility.","remediation":"Separate self-update logic from the browser QA skill, or require explicit interactive approval with transparent release source validation before any upgrade action. Pin trusted release sources, verify signatures or hashes, and document network/update behavior clearly.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"CHANGELOG.md","start_line":5,"end_line":12}]},{"id":"RA2","finding_id":"finding-c6c30fa1952c44fb809c183318121285","category":"Rogue Agent","pattern":"Session Persistence","severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.6,"location":{"file":"CLAUDE.md","start_line":84,"end_line":null},"finding":"write numbered decision steps: \"1. If X, do Y. 2. Otherwise, do Z.\"\n\n## Browser interaction\n\nWhen you need to interact with a browser (QA, dogfooding, cookie setup), use the\n`/browse` skill or run the","explanation":"Skill establishes unauthorized persistence across sessions via cron jobs, startup scripts, or state files. Session persistence allows an attacker to maintain access beyond the current interaction.","remediation":"Remove any persistence mechanisms (cron jobs, startup scripts, state files). Skills should not maintain state across sessions without explicit user consent.","code_snippet":"- **Keep bash blocks self-contained.** Each code block should work independently.\n  If a block needs context from a previous step, restate it in the prose above.\n- **Express conditionals as English.** Instead of nested `if/elif/else` in bash,\n  write numbered decision steps: \"1. If X, do Y. 2. Otherwise, do Z.\"\n\n## Browser interaction\n","intent":null,"tags":["Rogue Agent","llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"b0a8a5ab4a87466866f3d75e9fc79f6af2ce37e4526cc2fe2c66fcac1436d7c7","occurrences":[{"file":"CLAUDE.md","start_line":84,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"AS3","finding_id":"finding-569064f11be94295840ebe9aa99451f8","category":"Agent Snooping","pattern":"Skill Enumeration","severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.85,"location":{"file":"CLAUDE.md","start_line":100,"end_line":null},"finding":"ls -la .claude/skills","explanation":"Skill enumerates or reads other installed skills. Access to other skills' SKILL.md files or the skills directory reveals prompt instructions, capabilities, and secrets that should be invisible to peer skills.","remediation":"Remove all code or instructions that list or read other skills' files or directories. Skills should operate independently; cross-skill access is a privilege escalation.","code_snippet":"great for rapid iteration, risky during big refactors where half-written skills\ncould break other Claude Code sessions using gstack concurrently.\n\n**Check once per session:** Run `ls -la .claude/skills/gstack` to see if it's a\nsymlink or a real copy. If it's a symlink to your working directory, be aware that:\n- Template changes + `bun run gen:skill-docs` immediately affect all gstack invocations\n- Breaking changes to SKILL.md.tmpl files can break concurrent gstack sessions","intent":null,"tags":["Agent Snooping","llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"d24d941a86aa6c39f953ef9fe42a521d3c9f0c1d5b35464804c367282cb497c5","occurrences":[{"file":"CLAUDE.md","start_line":100,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"SQP-2","finding_id":"finding-ecd7ecf4504047d193d8de24632b7919","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.91,"location":{"file":"CLAUDE.md","start_line":142,"end_line":143},"finding":null,"explanation":"The documentation instructs users to run `git reset --hard origin/main` in the active skill directory without any warning that it will irrevocably discard local changes. In a live skill-development environment, this can cause accidental data loss or wipe uncommitted work if the command is copied mechanically by an agent or user.","remediation":"Add an explicit warning that `git reset --hard` destroys uncommitted local changes and should only be used after confirming the target directory and clean-state intent. Prefer safer guidance such as checking `git status` first, offering `git stash`/backup steps, or using a non-destructive update flow unless a hard reset is strictly required.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"CLAUDE.md","start_line":142,"end_line":143}]},{"id":"AS3","finding_id":"finding-00e6f94b72e54204a5a4159b99fadaf5","category":"Agent Snooping","pattern":"Skill Enumeration","severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.85,"location":{"file":"CONTRIBUTING.md","start_line":7,"end_line":null},"finding":"ls are Markdown files that Claude Code discovers from a `skills/` directory. Normally they live at `~/.claude/skills","explanation":"Skill enumerates or reads other installed skills. Access to other skills' SKILL.md files or the skills directory reveals prompt instructions, capabilities, and secrets that should be invisible to peer skills.","remediation":"Remove all code or instructions that list or read other skills' files or directories. Skills should operate independently; cross-skill access is a privilege escalation.","code_snippet":"\n## Quick start\n\ngstack skills are Markdown files that Claude Code discovers from a `skills/` directory. Normally they live at `~/.claude/skills/gstack/` (your global install). But when you're developing gstack itself, you want Claude Code to use the skills *in your working tree* — so edits take effect instantly without copying or deploying anything.\n\nThat's what dev mode does. It symlinks your repo into the local `.claude/skills/` directory so Claude Code reads skills straight from your checkout.\n","intent":null,"tags":["Agent Snooping","llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"6cd8b55d2e149a73630779c2238805a53b6e2721783d70a48d157ff23a7db6fa","occurrences":[{"file":"CONTRIBUTING.md","start_line":7,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"SQP-2","finding_id":"finding-c213fa18682f4829864e2577defbe007","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.92,"location":{"file":"CONTRIBUTING.md","start_line":25,"end_line":29},"finding":null,"explanation":"Contributor mode explicitly says the tool will automatically reflect on usage and write reports with 'what happened' and repro steps to a local log directory, but it does not warn contributors to avoid sensitive prompts, secrets, customer data, or internal URLs. Automatic workflow logging can capture confidential context during normal use and persist it on disk, increasing the chance of later disclosure.","remediation":"Add an explicit privacy warning in this section stating that contributor logs may contain sensitive workflow data and should not be enabled in sensitive environments without review. Document what fields are logged, provide redaction/exclusion controls, and default to sanitizing secrets, tokens, cookies, and user-provided content before writing logs.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"CONTRIBUTING.md","start_line":25,"end_line":29}]},{"id":"SQP-2","finding_id":"finding-54807535a2ad424d80092b765b93edee","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.95,"location":{"file":"CONTRIBUTING.md","start_line":232,"end_line":237},"finding":null,"explanation":"The Conductor workspace documentation states that `.env` is copied from the main worktree into each workspace so API keys carry over, but it does not warn that this propagates credentials into multiple filesystem locations. Credential duplication expands the attack surface and can leave secrets behind in archived or less-protected workspaces.","remediation":"Add a warning that workspace setup copies secrets into each worktree and explain the security implications. Prefer ephemeral environment injection over copying `.env`, or at minimum restrict permissions, avoid archiving secret-bearing workspaces, and provide a cleanup step that securely removes copied credential files.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"CONTRIBUTING.md","start_line":232,"end_line":237}]},{"id":"AS3","finding_id":"finding-04435e43d20d44b4ba16954d927104fc","category":"Agent Snooping","pattern":"Skill Enumeration","severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.85,"location":{"file":"CONTRIBUTING.md","start_line":244,"end_line":null},"finding":"ls take priority over `~/.claude/skills","explanation":"Skill enumerates or reads other installed skills. Access to other skills' SKILL.md files or the skills directory reveals prompt instructions, capabilities, and secrets that should be invisible to peer skills.","remediation":"Remove all code or instructions that list or read other skills' files or directories. Skills should operate independently; cross-skill access is a privilege escalation.","code_snippet":"- **SKILL.md files are generated.** Edit the `.tmpl` template, not the `.md`. Run `bun run gen:skill-docs` to regenerate.\n- **TODOS.md is the unified backlog.** Organized by skill/component with P0-P4 priorities. `/ship` auto-detects completed items. All planning/review/retro skills read it for context.\n- **Browse source changes need a rebuild.** If you touch `browse/src/*.ts`, run `bun run build`.\n- **Dev mode shadows your global install.** Project-local skills take priority over `~/.claude/skills/gstack`. `bin/dev-teardown` restores the global one.\n- **Conductor workspaces are independent.** Each workspace is its own git worktree. `bin/dev-setup` runs automatically via `conductor.json`.\n- **`.env` propagates across worktrees.** Set it once in the main repo, all Conductor workspaces get it.\n- **`.claude/skills/` is gitignored.** The symlinks never get committed.","intent":null,"tags":["Agent Snooping","llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"6f0c1c1b23525944a1e4eab7cf77c0de3d37b0f72491ef2aabe064658aba174e","occurrences":[{"file":"CONTRIBUTING.md","start_line":244,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"AS3","finding_id":"finding-c9b449811f544d42a7f02182b75d7822","category":"Agent Snooping","pattern":"Skill Enumeration","severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.85,"location":{"file":"CONTRIBUTING.md","start_line":271,"end_line":null},"finding":"ls back to `~/.claude/skills","explanation":"Skill enumerates or reads other installed skills. Access to other skills' SKILL.md files or the skills directory reveals prompt instructions, capabilities, and secrets that should be invisible to peer skills.","remediation":"Remove all code or instructions that list or read other skills' files or directories. Skills should operate independently; cross-skill access is a privilege escalation.","code_snippet":"rm .claude/skills/gstack\n```\n\nClaude Code falls back to `~/.claude/skills/gstack/` automatically.\n\n### Alternative: point your global install at a branch\n","intent":null,"tags":["Agent Snooping","llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"01b8b71f8c05cc3458844b413c5d197ee6799b1355abe50bf67a3628f1acb9c2","occurrences":[{"file":"CONTRIBUTING.md","start_line":271,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"RA2","finding_id":"finding-1319fbc84bac4ea58117e0178a3cce92","category":"Rogue Agent","pattern":"Session Persistence","severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.6,"location":{"file":"README.md","start_line":38,"end_line":null},"finding":"create\n       the listing from it.\n\nYou:   /plan-ceo-review\n\nClaude: \"Photo upload\" is not the feature. The real job is helping sellers\n        create listings that actually sell. Here's what the 10-s","explanation":"Skill establishes unauthorized persistence across sessions via cron jobs, startup scripts, or state files. Session persistence allows an attacker to maintain access beyond the current interaction.","remediation":"Remove any persistence mechanisms (cron jobs, startup scripts, state files). Skills should not maintain state across sessions without explicit user consent.","code_snippet":"```\nYou:   [enter plan mode, describe the feature]\n       I want to add seller photo upload to the listing app. Sellers\n       should be able to upload a photo of their item and we create\n       the listing from it.\n\nYou:   /plan-ceo-review","intent":null,"tags":["Rogue Agent","llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"2bb864938a060617525bb4b1c237592db363272e25cb8baf9fdfc34d52784c26","occurrences":[{"file":"README.md","start_line":38,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"SQP-2","finding_id":"finding-73fa343080d04dbc962b7f36864f1de6","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.93,"location":{"file":"README.md","start_line":110,"end_line":114},"finding":null,"explanation":"The install instructions tell users to recursively copy files into a project and delete the embedded .git directory without an explicit overwrite/destructive-action warning. In practice this can clobber an existing .claude/skills/gstack directory or local modifications, causing loss of project files or unexpected repository state changes.","remediation":"Add a warning that the command overwrites an existing destination and removes .git metadata from the copied tree. Recommend checking whether .claude/skills/gstack already exists, backing it up first, and using safer commands such as prompting before overwrite or installing into a fresh directory.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"README.md","start_line":110,"end_line":114}]},{"id":"AS3","finding_id":"finding-1f06426e270b46be819ab4e8718bd8d1","category":"Agent Snooping","pattern":"Skill Enumeration","severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.85,"location":{"file":"README.md","start_line":112,"end_line":null},"finding":"ls Claude that if gstack skills aren't working, run `cd .claude/skills","explanation":"Skill enumerates or reads other installed skills. Access to other skills' SKILL.md files or the skills directory reveals prompt instructions, capabilities, and secrets that should be invisible to peer skills.","remediation":"Remove all code or instructions that list or read other skills' files or directories. Skills should operate independently; cross-skill access is a privilege escalation.","code_snippet":"\n### Step 2: Add to your repo so teammates get it (optional)\n\n> Add gstack to this project: run `cp -Rf ~/.claude/skills/gstack .claude/skills/gstack && rm -rf .claude/skills/gstack/.git && cd .claude/skills/gstack && ./setup` then add a \"gstack\" section to this project's CLAUDE.md that says to use the /browse skill from gstack for all web browsing, never use mcp\\_\\_claude-in-chrome\\_\\_\\* tools, lists the available skills: /plan-ceo-review, /plan-eng-review, /review, /ship, /browse, /qa, /setup-browser-cookies, /retro, /document-release, and tells Claude that if gstack skills aren't working, run `cd .claude/skills/gstack && ./setup` to build the binary and register skills.\n\nReal files get committed to your repo (not a submodule), so `git clone` just works. The binary and node\\_modules are gitignored — teammates just need to run `cd .claude/skills/gstack && ./setup` once to build (or `/browse` handles it automatically on first use).\n","intent":null,"tags":["Agent Snooping","llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"e282565b7c07d73471331eb0519b3708a14a0b450511aa822c9b978972ef80b7","occurrences":[{"file":"README.md","start_line":112,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"RA2","finding_id":"finding-fc312ab123a044799c4238aa164a49e8","category":"Rogue Agent","pattern":"Session Persistence","severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.6,"location":{"file":"README.md","start_line":181,"end_line":null},"finding":"create a listing that actually sells.\n\nIf that is the real job, the whole plan changes.\n\nNow the model should ask:\n\n* Can we identify the product from the photo?\n* Can we infer the SKU or model number","explanation":"Skill establishes unauthorized persistence across sessions via cron jobs, startup scripts, or state files. Session persistence allows an attacker to maintain access beyond the current interaction.","remediation":"Remove any persistence mechanisms (cron jobs, startup scripts, state files). Skills should not maintain state across sessions without explicit user consent.","code_snippet":"\nThat is not the real product.\n\nIn `/plan-ceo-review`, I want the model to ask whether \"photo upload\" is even the feature. Maybe the real feature is helping someone create a listing that actually sells.\n\nIf that is the real job, the whole plan changes.\n","intent":null,"tags":["Rogue Agent","llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"48852d83f3e9bc4bcd57981a8c4bb8fe831f40035114dfeeb3c59836508d96bb","occurrences":[{"file":"README.md","start_line":181,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"SQP-2","finding_id":"finding-0ae4e123d8254cccb69459a42c264c0e","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.92,"location":{"file":"README.md","start_line":529,"end_line":555},"finding":null,"explanation":"The README promotes importing real browser cookies into a persistent headless session to access authenticated pages, but the warning is limited and does not clearly spell out the privacy and account-takeover risk of copying live authenticated sessions. If users import sensitive production or personal-account cookies, the tool gains access equivalent to the logged-in browser session, which can expose data or enable unintended actions.","remediation":"Add an explicit high-visibility warning near the feature description and usage examples stating that imported cookies represent live authenticated sessions, should only be used for accounts/environments the user intends to expose to the tool, and should never be used for highly sensitive production/admin accounts without approval. Document storage, retention, scope, and how to clear imported session state after testing.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"README.md","start_line":529,"end_line":555}]},{"id":"SQP-2","finding_id":"finding-fc9c9d670aae4caaaba8ffc42f2f7f11","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.91,"location":{"file":"README.md","start_line":637,"end_line":641},"finding":null,"explanation":"The uninstall instructions include recursive deletion commands for user and project directories, but they are presented as a paste-ready block without a clear destructive-operation warning. Users may run them blindly in an agent context, leading to unintended deletion of local customizations or neighboring files if paths are edited incorrectly.","remediation":"Precede uninstall commands with an explicit destructive warning and a note to verify paths before execution. Provide a safer step-by-step uninstall flow, such as listing targets first, prompting for confirmation, or moving directories to a backup location before permanent deletion.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"README.md","start_line":637,"end_line":641}]},{"id":"RA2","finding_id":"finding-c078d34b99ed40e184d7c23445aab3d9","category":"Rogue Agent","pattern":"Session Persistence","severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.89,"location":{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":24,"end_line":null},"finding":"mkdir -p ~/.gstack/sessions\ntouch ~/.gstack/sessions/\"$PPID\"\n_SESSIONS=$(find ~/.gstack","explanation":"The skill creates persistent session markers under `~/.gstack/sessions` and preserves browser state across invocations. Persistent state can increase privacy and security risk because cookies, tabs, and workflow context may remain available longer than users expect, enabling unintended reuse or cross-task contamination.","remediation":"Disclose persistence prominently, add easy session-clear commands, shorten retention by default, and isolate browser/session state per task or per domain when feasible.","code_snippet":"```bash\n_UPD=$(~/.claude/skills/gstack/bin/gstack-update-check 2>/dev/null || .claude/skills/gstack/bin/gstack-update-check 2>/dev/null || true)\n[ -n \"$_UPD\" ] && echo \"$_UPD\" || true\nmkdir -p ~/.gstack/sessions\ntouch ~/.gstack/sessions/\"$PPID\"\n_SESSIONS=$(find ~/.gstack/sessions -mmin -120 -type f 2>/dev/null | wc -l | tr -d ' ')\nfind ~/.gstack/sessions -mmin +120 -type f -delete 2>/dev/null || true","intent":null,"tags":["Rogue Agent"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"5e808e4c235a730213d17543b3ecd63b866e66e9ee00e0707fa038544ff15826","occurrences":[{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":24,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"RA2","finding_id":"finding-d8fc5a05bf444ca6b8423688ebd16857","category":"Rogue Agent","pattern":"Session Persistence","severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.86,"location":{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":33,"end_line":null},"finding":"write snooze state if declined). If `JUST_UPGRADED <from> <to>`: tell user \"Running gstack v{to} (just updated!)\" and continue.\n\n## AskUserQuestion Format\n\n**ALWAYS follow this structure for every Ask","explanation":"The inline upgrade flow writes snooze state and carries upgrade/session behavior across runs, which introduces retained state outside the immediate task. Persistent upgrade state is less severe than cookie handling, but it still expands the surface for hidden behavior and surprises users about what survives between sessions.","remediation":"Document persisted upgrade/snooze state clearly and provide a way to inspect/reset it. Keep persisted metadata minimal and segregated from browsing/authentication state.","code_snippet":"echo \"BRANCH: $_BRANCH\"\n```\n\nIf output shows `UPGRADE_AVAILABLE <old> <new>`: read `~/.claude/skills/gstack/gstack-upgrade/SKILL.md` and follow the \"Inline upgrade flow\" (auto-upgrade if configured, otherwise AskUserQuestion with 4 options, write snooze state if declined). If `JUST_UPGRADED <from> <to>`: tell user \"Running gstack v{to} (just updated!)\" and continue.\n\n## AskUserQuestion Format\n","intent":null,"tags":["Rogue Agent"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"897ec9f9d58b2e14e9f5e1b059f4c1cef2b28ccbca8277d62d296f27bf766e10","occurrences":[{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":33,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"SQP-2","finding_id":"finding-acb4ad96da1346f8a6ff1c6bf0202b45","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.98,"location":{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":103,"end_line":106},"finding":null,"explanation":"The skill instructs downloading and piping a remote installer script directly into `bash`, which is a classic supply-chain risk. If the remote endpoint, TLS trust, or distribution channel is compromised, arbitrary code will execute on the user’s machine with the agent’s privileges.","remediation":"Never use `curl | bash` in skill instructions. Pin versions, provide checksum/signature verification, direct users to manual installation steps, or install from trusted package managers with integrity validation.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":103,"end_line":106}]},{"id":"SDI-2","finding_id":"finding-de3fd4b501a44339ba0e475bc3aa7927","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.99,"location":{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":241,"end_line":249},"finding":null,"explanation":"The skill explicitly supports importing cookies from real local browsers to test authenticated pages. That is dangerous because browser cookies are active session secrets; importing them into automation can enable account access, session replay, or unintended actions as the user across sensitive sites.","remediation":"Add an explicit privacy/security warning before any browser-cookie import feature. Require domain-specific consent, prefer test accounts over real sessions, and disable broad browser-cookie import by default.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":241,"end_line":249}]},{"id":"SQP-2","finding_id":"finding-87c1ed40cd84433a8fc1be64c6f9c085","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.98,"location":{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":241,"end_line":253},"finding":null,"explanation":"The documentation encourages importing real browser cookies for authenticated testing but does not prominently warn that these cookies are equivalent to live login tokens. Without strong notice, users may expose personal or production sessions to automation and any downstream logging or misuse.","remediation":"Add a bold security warning near the feature, explain that cookies are live session credentials, and recommend dedicated test accounts. Require explicit confirmation showing the target domain and the risks before import.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":241,"end_line":253}]},{"id":"SDI-2","finding_id":"finding-5d0b9311bac3437995123bd5ba7ef042","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.96,"location":{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":394,"end_line":396},"finding":null,"explanation":"Arbitrary JavaScript execution against loaded pages goes beyond ordinary click/fill/screenshot testing and can directly manipulate application state. In an authenticated session, this could submit forms, alter data, read DOM-exposed secrets, or trigger privileged actions invisibly.","remediation":"Restrict `js`/`eval` to explicit debug mode, log usage prominently, and warn users before running page-context code on authenticated or production sites. Where possible, replace with narrower safe assertions APIs.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":394,"end_line":396}]},{"id":"RA2","finding_id":"finding-f08ef39349504596a90897b32e929551","category":"Rogue Agent","pattern":"Session Persistence","severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.6,"location":{"file":"TODOS.md","start_line":192,"end_line":null},"finding":"create pull request review comments.\n\n**Why:** Line-level annotations are more actionable than top-level comments. The PR thread becomes a line-by-line conversation between Greptile, Claude, and human","explanation":"Skill establishes unauthorized persistence across sessions via cron jobs, startup scripts, or state files. Session persistence allows an attacker to maintain access beyond the current interaction.","remediation":"Remove any persistence mechanisms (cron jobs, startup scripts, state files). Skills should not maintain state across sessions without explicit user consent.","code_snippet":"\n### Inline PR annotations\n\n**What:** /ship and /review post inline review comments at specific file:line locations using `gh api` to create pull request review comments.\n\n**Why:** Line-level annotations are more actionable than top-level comments. The PR thread becomes a line-by-line conversation between Greptile, Claude, and human reviewers.\n","intent":null,"tags":["Rogue Agent","llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"63fd0389681bdea88a0b3bea294f44fd29c168eba0a54114dd27b8bc8c5c9b44","occurrences":[{"file":"TODOS.md","start_line":192,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"SQP-2","finding_id":"finding-a5e1cd9eece3478d9a29cf36d29a4c4e","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.91,"location":{"file":"TODOS.md","start_line":326,"end_line":326},"finding":null,"explanation":"This is a markdown file, so SQP-2 applies to descriptions that omit warnings about behaviors affecting privacy or system integrity. The TODO explicitly states that `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` is required in CI secrets, but provides no caution about secret management, exposure risks in logs/artifacts, or least-privilege handling.","remediation":"Add a brief warning in the task description that CI secrets will be required and must be configured securely, must not be printed to logs or embedded in artifacts, and should use least-privilege/project-scoped credentials where possible.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":["llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"TODOS.md","start_line":326,"end_line":326}]},{"id":"SQP-2","finding_id":"finding-f246ea99e3a64fb8bcbae1275675e21a","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.88,"location":{"file":"TODOS.md","start_line":381,"end_line":385},"finding":null,"explanation":"For markdown files, SQP-2 covers omitted warnings about behaviors that can affect user data or system integrity. These lines describe automatically reading another skill and executing a doc update workflow after creating a PR, but do not warn that `/ship` would trigger extra automated edits/commits beyond its current scope.","remediation":"Add an explicit note that `/ship` would automatically perform additional documentation changes after PR creation, and recommend user visibility or confirmation before applying or committing those updates.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":["llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"TODOS.md","start_line":381,"end_line":385}]},{"id":"RA2","finding_id":"finding-5dfcac75580b48658a719ddb69f20ab8","category":"Rogue Agent","pattern":"Session Persistence","severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.83,"location":{"file":"browse/SKILL.md","start_line":24,"end_line":null},"finding":"mkdir -p ~/.gstack/sessions\ntouch ~/.gstack/sessions/\"$PPID\"\n_SESSIONS=$(find ~/.gstack","explanation":"The preamble creates and maintains session marker files under the user's home directory, indicating cross-invocation state tracking. While not inherently malicious, persistent local state increases privacy exposure and can support unintended correlation of sessions or stale state influencing later runs.","remediation":"Document the session-tracking behavior clearly, minimize retained data, and provide a cleanup/reset mechanism. Store only what is operationally necessary, use short retention by default, and keep state isolated to the minimum scope needed.","code_snippet":"```bash\n_UPD=$(~/.claude/skills/gstack/bin/gstack-update-check 2>/dev/null || .claude/skills/gstack/bin/gstack-update-check 2>/dev/null || true)\n[ -n \"$_UPD\" ] && echo \"$_UPD\" || true\nmkdir -p ~/.gstack/sessions\ntouch ~/.gstack/sessions/\"$PPID\"\n_SESSIONS=$(find ~/.gstack/sessions -mmin -120 -type f 2>/dev/null | wc -l | tr -d ' ')\nfind ~/.gstack/sessions -mmin +120 -type f -delete 2>/dev/null || true","intent":null,"tags":["Rogue Agent"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"5e808e4c235a730213d17543b3ecd63b866e66e9ee00e0707fa038544ff15826","occurrences":[{"file":"browse/SKILL.md","start_line":24,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"RA2","finding_id":"finding-f82ef6fd42dd41ae9c6579056bd19ac9","category":"Rogue Agent","pattern":"Session Persistence","severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.74,"location":{"file":"browse/SKILL.md","start_line":33,"end_line":null},"finding":"write snooze state if declined). If `JUST_UPGRADED <from> <to>`: tell user \"Running gstack v{to} (just updated!)\" and continue.\n\n## AskUserQuestion Format\n\n**ALWAYS follow this structure for every Ask","explanation":"The inline upgrade flow includes writing snooze state and persisting upgrade-related decisions, which is another form of cross-run state retention. This is lower risk than credential access, but it still introduces hidden persistent behavior that users may not expect from a QA browsing skill.","remediation":"Disclose upgrade-state persistence explicitly and keep such state minimal, time-bounded, and easy to clear. Avoid automatic persistence where not necessary, or ask for one-time consent before storing preference state.","code_snippet":"echo \"BRANCH: $_BRANCH\"\n```\n\nIf output shows `UPGRADE_AVAILABLE <old> <new>`: read `~/.claude/skills/gstack/gstack-upgrade/SKILL.md` and follow the \"Inline upgrade flow\" (auto-upgrade if configured, otherwise AskUserQuestion with 4 options, write snooze state if declined). If `JUST_UPGRADED <from> <to>`: tell user \"Running gstack v{to} (just updated!)\" and continue.\n\n## AskUserQuestion Format\n","intent":null,"tags":["Rogue Agent"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"897ec9f9d58b2e14e9f5e1b059f4c1cef2b28ccbca8277d62d296f27bf766e10","occurrences":[{"file":"browse/SKILL.md","start_line":33,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"SQP-2","finding_id":"finding-f1709227ef2841c7a94a0e19dbf3227a","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.9,"location":{"file":"browse/SKILL.md","start_line":86,"end_line":87},"finding":null,"explanation":"The skill explicitly states that cookies, tabs, and login sessions persist between calls but does not warn about privacy or data carryover. This can cause accidental reuse of prior authentication state, leakage between tasks, and mistaken actions in privileged sessions when users assume each run is isolated.","remediation":"Add prominent warnings that browser state persists across runs, explain the privacy implications, and provide simple commands to inspect, clear, or reset state. Consider defaulting to isolated sessions per task unless the user explicitly opts into persistence.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"browse/SKILL.md","start_line":86,"end_line":87}]},{"id":"SQP-2","finding_id":"finding-d4061a647df84fce93ebe974a441212b","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.96,"location":{"file":"browse/SKILL.md","start_line":103,"end_line":106},"finding":null,"explanation":"The setup path includes a remote install script piped to bash without any safety warning, verification step, or trust boundary explanation. Even when framed as a convenience install, this normalizes unsafe execution of network-fetched shell code and materially increases compromise risk.","remediation":"Replace the curl-to-bash instruction with safer install guidance, such as package-manager commands, downloadable signed artifacts, or a separately reviewed script. Add an explicit warning about executing remote scripts and require informed consent before any installation step.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"browse/SKILL.md","start_line":103,"end_line":106}]},{"id":"SDI-2","finding_id":"finding-e8a8673df68a45e687cf2a8d6fa22090","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.97,"location":{"file":"browse/SKILL.md","start_line":106,"end_line":106},"finding":null,"explanation":"The skill instructs installing Bun by piping a remotely fetched script directly into bash, which executes unreviewed code from the network with the user's privileges. That behavior is broader than the stated browsing/QA purpose and creates a software supply-chain execution path if the remote host, transport, or install script is compromised.","remediation":"Remove curl-to-shell installation from the skill. Require Bun to be preinstalled via trusted package managers or documented manual steps, and if setup is unavoidable, present the exact script for user review, verify checksums/signatures, and require explicit user confirmation before any install action.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"browse/SKILL.md","start_line":106,"end_line":106}]},{"id":"SQP-2","finding_id":"finding-9405b964029842ca88a3a0002d1287e9","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.94,"location":{"file":"browse/src/browser-manager.ts","start_line":396,"end_line":412},"finding":null,"explanation":"The page-level dialog handler automatically accepts or dismisses alert/confirm/prompt dialogs, and may even submit a preconfigured prompt value, without requiring an explicit per-action confirmation from the caller. In a headless browsing skill that can navigate arbitrary sites and exercise user flows, this can silently bypass safety interstitials, destructive confirms, or workflow checks, causing unintended state-changing actions on target applications.","remediation":"Make dialog handling explicit and opt-in at the command or operation level rather than global-by-default. Surface dialog events to the caller with clear warnings, default to pausing or dismissing unless a specific action authorizes acceptance, and restrict prompt text injection to narrowly scoped operations with audit logging.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"browse/src/browser-manager.ts","start_line":396,"end_line":412}]},{"id":"SDI-2","finding_id":"finding-d30fe2d68d124a7094049433f3631a03","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.91,"location":{"file":"browse/src/cli.ts","start_line":101,"end_line":129},"finding":null,"explanation":"The legacy cleanup scans shared /tmp files, trusts JSON content from those files, and may send SIGTERM to any live PID whose command line merely contains 'bun' or 'server.ts'. In a multi-process or multi-user environment, that heuristic is too broad and can terminate unrelated local processes, creating a denial-of-service condition outside the tool's stated QA-browser scope.","remediation":"Do not kill processes based on untrusted /tmp metadata plus substring command matching. Restrict cleanup to state files owned by the current user, verify an exact process identity using a stronger marker (for example a dedicated pidfile path, startup token, or command-line signature unique to this server), and prefer deleting legacy files without signaling when ownership cannot be proven.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"browse/src/cli.ts","start_line":101,"end_line":129}]},{"id":"SQP-2","finding_id":"finding-ddeda4328e39449eaeedadfb6ca01fd4","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.89,"location":{"file":"browse/src/commands.ts","start_line":59,"end_line":75},"finding":null,"explanation":"This TypeScript file is a code file, so SQP-2 applies to safety-relevant operations described here. The entries for `cookies`, `storage`, `cookie`, `cookie-import`, and `cookie-import-browser` expose or modify authentication/session data, but the file provides only terse descriptions and no warning comment or user-facing disclosure about the privacy/security impact of reading, setting, or importing browser cookies and storage.","remediation":"Add explicit warning text in the command descriptions or nearby comments stating that these commands can read, modify, or import sensitive session data and may affect authenticated state. If the warning is documented elsewhere, reference that documentation clearly from this registry.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":["llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"browse/src/commands.ts","start_line":59,"end_line":75}]},{"id":"MP2","finding_id":"finding-d1f9ee0ff308453c8fd1fa3108b0b9f1","category":"Memory Poisoning","pattern":"Context Window Stuffing","severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.85,"location":{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":374,"end_line":null},"finding":"Fill input","explanation":"Skill attempts to fill the context window with filler content, displacing legitimate instructions and safety constraints. This can degrade agent performance or bypass safety boundaries.","remediation":"Implement context-window management that detects and rejects padding or stuffing attempts. Prioritize system instructions over user-injected content.","code_snippet":"  'perf':    { category: 'Inspection', description: 'Page load timings' },\n  // Interaction\n  'click':   { category: 'Interaction', description: 'Click element', usage: 'click <sel>' },\n  'fill':    { category: 'Interaction', description: 'Fill input', usage: 'fill <sel> <val>' },\n  'select':  { category: 'Interaction', description: 'Select dropdown option by value, label, or visible text', usage: 'select <sel> <val>' },\n  'hover':   { category: 'Interaction', description: 'Hover element', usage: 'hover <sel>' },\n  'type':    { category: 'Interaction', description: 'Type into focused element', usage: 'type <text>' },","intent":null,"tags":["Memory Poisoning","llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"7a90427092f85fe236c42657231437965bc0